  "answer": "This is a known Keyboard Manager limitation. When you remap Alt+Tab to Disable, only that exact two-key combination is blocked. If you hold another key such as W at the same time, the combination no longer matches the remap. Windows then handles it as a normal Alt+Tab. Several earlier reports describe the same behavior (#25034, #32164, #27991). They were closed as duplicates of #21850, which is the tracking issue. #21850 asks for a 'fallthrough' checkbox so that a disabled shortcut stays blocked when extra keys are pressed. I found no fix or full workaround in those tickets, so I can't promise one. Upvote #21850 to help prioritize it. One partial workaround is to remap the Tab key itself, but that also removes Tab everywhere. A reporter in #32164 noted this and didn't want to lose Tab."
